    "turn_text": "yes unfortunate i mean that you know who is is doing every everything that he is doing to try to preserve and protect the only democracy in the middle east uh... he would be doing exactly if they were that you know who there it would be whoever would be in the next uh... prime minister would be doing the same thing to protect his real in this time of need so uh... one thing uh... that that they fail to understand that they try to blame Everything in Israel and Netanyahu, which is a fool there. And look, Israel is more unified now than ever. They have a vibrant democracy. They have robust disagreements and debates. on policy, but I think he is mostly, people mostly agree with what he's trying to do to try to come up with the peaceful solution without getting completely dissolved by what's going on with Hamas and what's happening in Gaza and the continued attacks being sponsored by Iran, which is a huge, I mean, this is incredible. I've been talking about this for a long time. Iran continues to be emboldened by the weak policies coming from Biden and Harris. And they're going to continue to make it very difficult in the Middle East for a lot of people seeking democracy, undermining the Abraham Accords, the historic Accords that President Trump put in place, that we're starting to give people in the Middle East hope for peace and prosperity. Netanyahu's speech laid bare the truth, the facts. He dispels a lot of the mythology and a lot of the false propaganda that's coming out of Hamas. that is funded by Iran that a lot of these protesters are believing in, they're gullible, they don't want to participate in real fact-finding and understand what's going on. I thought it was an excellent speech, it was historic. He's a courageous leader, you don't have to agree with 100% of his policies. He's trying to stand up for the Israeli people, but he also was standing up for America, and I thought that was a very important point that he made. He is fighting our war there, he is standing up to radicalism, and he made a great analogy between what was happening in World War II under Nazi Germany when people turned their backs and did not stand up to the Nazis. We need to do that today. We need to stand up to these radical anti-Semitic people who are also anti-American. And so I think it was a really important speech. It was great to get to speak with him actually after his appearance. On the floor, I got a little moment with him with one of my real good friends and colleagues, David Kustoff from Tennessee, and also Governor Bill Lee of Tennessee and his wife. It was really great to just have our own little meeting with him just happen to be there at the right time and to talk to him about our strong work, standing up for Israel, and also standing up against Iran, who has created the unrest in the Middle East. That is the problem. A lot of these countries would love to be part of the Abraham Accords. Look at Saudi Arabia was on the cusp of joining the Abraham Accords, interested in changing everything that's happening in Saudi Arabia. A lot of the negatives have been turned around by their great ambassador, Princess Rima. She has done a lot to bring us forward in an area that really needs to get on the map, to bring prosperity, to bring human rights, and they're trying. But as long as Iran is continuing to cause violence, to fund proxies. Against other countries in the Middle East, we aren't going to resolve this problem. We're not going to see peace. We're not going to see prosperity. And this administration is so weak on Iran. It's really what is causing this prolonged and perpetual violence that we're seeing there.",
