  {
    "episode_id": 81194,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81194.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, thank you so much for mentioning this, Joe. Really, we're going to be sounding the alarm on this. I continue to hear from our volunteer firemen across this very, very expansive district. They are trying to put in place a law and a rule that's actually not been made permanent yet, but we're in the beginning stages of it that would not allow volunteer fire departments to have any trucks that are over 20 years old. If you've been to a volunteer fire department across any part of upstate New York, Most of them are over 20 years old and they spend a lot of time fixing and repairing them and keeping them up to date. This would be devastating to our fire service. It would threaten our public safety and their ability to answer respond to calls. This is something that we're fighting against. We're going to be going across the district to make sure that this law doesn't go into effect. And inspection is what needs to be the standard, not the age of the vehicle. If the vehicle is in perfect condition and it happens to be 21 years old, then it still should be an operation. And let me tell you something, these fire department members Many of these volunteers, they have a job by day and they do this by night. They answer the real call to serve to keep us safe. And many of them are modern day MacGyvers. They get out in a fix the equipment. They make it happen. They make it work. We owe a debt of gratitude to these people. And this would be devastating to those people who are doing a full day's work and then serving their community in a volunteer way to keep us safe. I can't think of anything more heroic than that. and we're going to be fighting to protect our volunteer fire departments across the across the district but thanks for asking that i hope that continues to get out there",
